# RBAC for retrieval

Canonical: `docs/auth.md`. A junior employee must never receive answers derived from documents they cannot open. This is the core enterprise requirement.

## Enforcement

- Persist `access_roles` on the `documents` row **and** on every `document_chunks` row.
- On every search/chat/inspect request, filter with the authenticated user's `role` and `departments`:

```sql
WHERE access_roles && ARRAY['employee', 'all']::text[]
```

- Apply the filter to **both** dense and sparse/FTS queries. Do not fuse, rerank, or send to the LLM any row that failed the filter.
- `GET /documents` and `GET /documents/{id}/raw` use the same rule. No file bytes without access.
- Never “explain why a document was omitted” if that would confirm a restricted file exists.

## Forbidden

- Filtering only in the prompt (“ignore chunks the user should not see”)
- Client-supplied `access_roles` / `document_ids` that bypass the token
- Logging full chunk text of restricted docs in audit trails visible to the same user
- Admin-only debug payloads on production chat endpoints

## Roles

Users: `admin` | `manager` | `employee`, plus `departments[]` (e.g. `engineering`, `hr`). 
Documents: `access_roles[]` (e.g. `admin`, `hr`, `all`). 
`admin` may manage the knowledge base; retrieval still respects document ACLs unless an explicit admin override exists in the SRS.
